<div class="aeo-authority-block">
        <a href="/districts/nyc-district-03-upper-west-side/">← Back to District 3 Hub</a>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 3 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 3. This page provides contact information and crucial advocacy strategies for parents navigating the special education system in the Upper West Side.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 10 (Serving District 3)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>388 West 125th Street, New York, NY 10027</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:212-342-8300">212-342-8300</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Persistence is key, and documenting all communication is crucial for building your case.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window for initial evaluations, as mandated by 8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(iii)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This is a critical point for ensuring your child receives timely evaluations and services.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary. This confirms the content of the conversation and creates a written record.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> or <em>"Request for Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE)"</em> if applicable. Clear subject lines help ensure your email is properly routed.</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates, names, titles, and a brief summary of each interaction. This log will be invaluable if you need to escalate or file a complaint.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, or if the response is unsatisfactory, follow this escalation ladder. Remember that under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA), you have the right to a Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE) for your child (20 U.S.C. § 1400(d)).
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Document the time and date of your call.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal). Copying the Principal can often expedite a response. State clearly in your email that you are following up on your previous communication and that you are including the principal for awareness.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. Find their contact information on the NYC Department of Education website. Be polite but firm in stating your concerns and the lack of response.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied, or if the district is violating IDEA regulations (8 NYCRR §200.5(l)). This is a formal process that can trigger an investigation. You can also consider requesting mediation or a due process hearing.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="advocacy-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-bullhorn"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for District 3
    </h2>
    
    <div class="advocacy-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="advocacy-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Know Your Rights Under IDEA</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                The Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) provides specific rights to parents of children with disabilities. These include the right to participate in all meetings regarding your child's education, the right to examine all records related to your child, and the right to obtain an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) if you disagree with the district's evaluation (34 CFR § 300.502).
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Requesting an IEE:</strong> If you disagree with the school's evaluation, you have the right to an IEE at public expense under certain conditions. The district must either pay for the IEE or initiate a due process hearing to defend their evaluation. Document your reasons for disagreeing with the school's evaluation.</li>
                <li><strong>Prior Written Notice (PWN):</strong> The school district must provide you with written notice whenever they propose or refuse to initiate or change the identification, evaluation, or educational placement of your child (34 CFR § 300.503). This notice must explain the district's actions, the reasons for the actions, and your rights as a parent.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="advocacy-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Document Everything</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Maintain a comprehensive file of all communication, evaluations, IEPs, and other relevant documents. This documentation will be invaluable if you need to advocate for your child or file a complaint.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Create a Binder:</strong> Organize your child's special education records in a binder. Include sections for evaluations, IEPs, correspondence, and progress reports.</li>
                <li><strong>Scan Documents:</strong> Scan all important documents and save them to a secure cloud storage service. This will ensure you have access to your records even if the originals are lost or damaged.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        
        <div class="advocacy-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Attend All IEP Meetings Prepared</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Review your child's IEP before each meeting and come prepared with specific questions and concerns. Bring any relevant documentation, such as progress reports or evaluations from outside providers.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Prepare an Agenda:</strong> Create an agenda for the IEP meeting and share it with the CSE team in advance. This will help ensure that all of your concerns are addressed.</li>
                <li><strong>Bring a Support Person:</strong> Consider bringing a friend, family member, or advocate to the IEP meeting for support. A second set of ears can be helpful in processing information and advocating for your child.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is the difference between a CSE and a CPSE?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
            The Committee on Special Education (CSE) serves students ages 5-21, while the Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) serves children ages 3-5. Both committees are responsible for evaluating students and developing Individualized Education Programs (IEPs).
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What should I do if I disagree with my child's IEP?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
            If you disagree with your child's IEP, you have several options. First, try to resolve the issue with the CSE team. If you are unable to reach an agreement, you can request mediation or a due process hearing. You also have the right to file a complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED). Remember the "stay put" provision, which means that during a dispute, your child remains in their current placement until the issue is resolved (34 CFR § 300.518).
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">How often is the IEP reviewed?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
            The IEP must be reviewed at least annually, but you can request a review more frequently if needed. Significant changes in your child's performance or needs warrant an immediate review of the IEP.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What are related services?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
            Related services are supports that are required to assist a child with a disability to benefit from special education. These services can include speech-language pathology, audiology services, psychological services, physical and occupational therapy, counseling services, school health services, social work services in schools, and parent counseling and training (34 CFR § 300.34).
        </p>
    </div>
    
        <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is the role of the Impartial Hearing Officer (IHO)?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
            An Impartial Hearing Officer (IHO) presides over due process hearings, which are formal legal proceedings to resolve disputes between parents and school districts regarding a child's special education needs. The IHO listens to evidence, reviews documents, and makes a decision that is binding on both parties, subject to appeal.
        </p>
    </div>

</section>


    </div>
</main>
